Let's trace how `style` and `classDef` are parsed and processed in `beautiful-mermaid`.

Let's look at the vulnerability description again:
"beautiful-mermaid affected versions before [redacted-version] contain an SVG attribute injection issue that can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) when rendering attacker-controlled Mermaid diagrams. User-controlled values from Mermaid style and classDef directives are interpolated into SVG attribute values without proper escaping, allowing crafted input to break out of an attribute context and inject arbitrary SVG elements/attributes into the rendered output. When the generated SVG is embedded in a web page, this can result in script execution in the context of the embedding origin."

Let's read this description carefully again:
"User-controlled values from Mermaid style and classDef directives are interpolated into SVG attribute values without proper escaping"

Let's think:
Where in the code are `style` and `classDef` directives parsed and interpolated?

Let's imagine how `style` and `classDef` directives are handled in `beautiful-mermaid`:

Suppose in `src/parser.ts` or `src/index.ts`:
When parsing `classDef`:
```ts
if (line.startsWith('classDef ')) {
  const match = line.match(/^classDef\s+(\w+)\s+(.+)$/);
  if (match) {
    const [, name, styles] = match;
    classDefs.set(name, styles);
  }
}
```
And `style`:
```ts
